  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal agencies obligated $1.4B in West Virginia in FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 25.6% year over year while agencies awarded 4,084 contracts at an average award size of $342K. The Department of Energy, the Department of Defense, and the Department of Veterans Affairs are the top buyers so far, with obligations of $283M, $264M, and $227M respectively. LEIDOS, INC., NORTHROP GRUMMAN SYSTEMS CORP, and SAWTOOTH CONSTRUCTION JV II LLC lead vendors, receiving $137M, $131M, and $60.7M respectively. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences is the largest industry by obligations at $169M, followed closely by Other Computer Related Services at $166M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $155M. Historical obligations show a peak at $2B in FY24 and a decline to $1.4B in FY26. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in West Virginia changed recently?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 25.6% year over year. Total obligations reached $1.4B in FY2026 to date, down from $1.9B in FY25 and $2B in FY24. Agencies awarded 4,084 contracts at an average award size of $342K, and current-year totals are partial since the fiscal year is still in progress."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors are driving spending in West Virginia?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy, the Department of Defense, and the Department of Veterans Affairs are the top buyers with $283M, $264M, and $227M in obligations. LEIDOS, INC., NORTHROP GRUMMAN SYSTEMS CORP, and SAWTOOTH CONSTRUCTION JV II LLC are the top vendors with $137M, $131M, and $60.7M in obligations. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences, Other Computer Related Services, and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction are the leading industries by obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
